      "text": "We start by defining the number of rows. Since the first row has 1 can and each subsequent row has 2 more cans than the row above it, the number of cans in each row forms an arithmetic sequence where the first term $a_1 = 1$ and the common difference $d = 2$.\n\nThe sum of the number of cans in the first $n$ rows can be expressed as:\n$$S_n = \\frac{n}{2}(2a_1 + (n - 1)d)$$\n\nThe total number of cans in the display is given as 100. We want to find $n$, so we set up the equation:\n$$100 = \\frac{n}{2}(2 \\cdot 1 + (n - 1) \\cdot 2)$$\n\nSimplifying the equation, we get:\n$$n(1 + 2(n - 1)) = 100$$\n$$n^2 - n - 100 = 0$$\n\nNow we need to solve this quadratic equation to find the value of $n$. We can use the quadratic formula:\n$$n = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}$$\n\nHere, $a = 1$, $b = -1$, and $c = -100$. Plugging these values into the quadratic formula, we get:\n$$n = \\frac{-(-1) \\pm \\sqrt{(-1)^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-100)}}{2 \\cdot 1}$$\n$$n = \\frac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{1 + 400}}{2}$$\n$$n = \\frac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{401}}{2}$$\n\nSince the number of rows must be a positive integer, we take the positive root:\n$$n = \\frac{1 + \\sqrt{401}}{2}$$\n$$n = 11$$\n\nSo, the display contains $n = \\boxed{11}$ rows.",
